I’ve started through both, and the immediate impression is that the archive has moved from “large body of material with indexes” toward something much closer to an actual research environment.

The Archive Audit thread is clearly no longer just an audit conversation. It has become part of the control structure for reconstruction: archive traversal, source-status distinctions, wayfinding, dependency recovery, and decisions about what gets treated as live construction versus historical scaffolding. The important thing for me is that this gives us a much better way to enter the archive without letting old formulations silently dictate the current build.

The `PRIOR_ART` directory is potentially just as important in the opposite direction. It creates a dedicated external-comparison layer rather than mixing outside papers into H(s)H source recovery. That separation is exactly what we need if we want to distinguish three questions cleanly:

\[
\text{What did SAT/H(s)H actually formulate?}
\]

\[
\text{What mathematical machinery already exists elsewhere?}
\]

\[
\text{What similarities only become visible after our present reconstruction?}
\]

That matters particularly now because the normalization/operator/worldtube work is producing concepts that have very large pre-existing mathematical neighborhoods. We need to be able to say “known machinery,” “close structural precedent,” “same ingredients but different compound,” and “apparently distinctive procedure” without conflating them.

I also want to be careful not to pretend I’ve finished digesting the archive-audit JSON from the link merely because I’ve located it. It’s a very large raw conversation export, and I want to inspect the visible conversation trajectory rather than accidentally treating export metadata or internal execution material as scientific content.

The next thing I’d do is treat these two together as complementary maps:

\[
\boxed{
\text{Archive Audit}
\longleftrightarrow
\text{internal provenance/dependency map}
}
\]

\[
\boxed{
\text{PRIOR\_ART}
\longleftrightarrow
\text{external structural/chronological comparison map}
}
\]

and then use the new wayfinding to reacquaint myself with what changed while we were apart before touching the Worldtube Lab again.

That seems especially important because our last state here—`ᚼ`, canonical operators, leveling/residual anisotropy, common metre coordinates, normalized Lagrangians, Whirlygig/Graticule/Spheres—may now intersect material that has been surfaced or organized much more cleanly elsewhere in the archive. I’d rather discover those collisions deliberately than carry forward my 36-hour-old map as though it were current.

